{"A":"Own a sequential radix-sort candidate: cache-friendly LSD passes with signed-order-safe keys, reused buffers, and tuned digit widths. Preserve case generation and exact output/MD5 bytes; do not hardcode sizes or results. Include the post-sort validation cost in measurements.","B":"Own a 4-core parallel radix-sort candidate. Use thread-local histograms, deterministic per-thread bucket offsets, disjoint scatters, and a sequential cutoff to avoid startup/clearing overhead. Avoid shared RNG/data races and validate tiny, duplicate, and large cases.","C":"Own an independent comparison-sort fallback: a robust iterative/introspective sorter with deterministic pivots, small-range handling, and only coarse-grain parallel tasks where profitable. Retain the generator exactly; pivot RNG state need not be reproduced after generation. Measure full-program performance versus radix approaches.","G":"Integrate only measured, correct candidates. Treat A/B/C as alternatives first; verify unchanged stdout and sorted native int bytes across valid sizes, then benchmark the complete program on the unchanged evaluator. A hybrid is acceptable only if its own measurement wins—do not assume gains compose.","analysis":"The sole baseline is correct at 69.945 ms mean over four cases on 4-core ARM64. The source deterministically generates integers, then spends work in recursive randomized Lomuto quicksort; only the final sorted array affects conditional MD5/output. Linear radix sorting is the main hypothesis, but the short baseline makes allocation, histogram clearing, thread launch, checksum, and the redundant sortedness scan material."}